{"name":"Baby Barn Owl","hex":"#c3c3b8","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baby","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very young human, particularly from birth to a couple of years old or until walking is fully mastered.","Any very young animal, especially a vertebrate; many species have specific names for their babies, such as kittens for the babies of cats, puppies for the babies of dogs, and chicks for the babies of birds. See Category:Baby animals for more."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Picked when small and immature (as in baby corn, baby potatoes).","Newest (overall, or in some group or state); most inexperienced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baby"},{"term":"barn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A building, often found on a farm, used for storage or keeping animals such as cattle.","A unit of surface area equal to 10 square metres."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To lay up in a barn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barn"},{"term":"owl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Old Welsh."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various birds of prey of the order Strigiformes, families Strigidae and Tytonidae, that are primarily nocturnal and have forward-looking, binocular vision, limited eye movement, and good hearing. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A person seen as having owl-like characteristics, especially appearing wise or serious, or being nocturnally active."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/owl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barn owl","blocks":[{"text":"The barn owls are owls in the genus Tyto, the most widely distributed genus of owls in the world. They are medium-sized owls with large heads and characteristic heart-shaped faces. They have long, strong legs with powerful talons. The term may be used to describe the whole family Tytonidae, which also includes the bay owls in the genus Phodilus.\nTyto is the largest genus of the Tytonidae and includes these species:"},{"items":["Three species that are sometimes considered to be a single species known as barn owl or common barn owl:\nWestern barn owl T. alba in Europe, Africa, and West Asia\nAmerican barn owl T. furcata in the Americas\nEastern barn owl T. javanica in Southeast Asia and Australasia","Western barn owl T. alba in Europe, Africa, and West Asia","American barn owl T. furcata in the Americas","Eastern barn owl T. javanica in Southeast Asia and Australasia","Andaman masked owl T. deroepstorffi endemic to the southern Andaman Islands","New Caledonian barn owl T. letocarti on the islands of New Caledonia in Melanesia, extinct","Rivero's barn owl T. riveroi in Cuba, extinct"]},{"items":["Western barn owl T. alba in Europe, Africa, and West Asia","American barn owl T. furcata in the Americas","Eastern barn owl T. javanica in Southeast Asia and Australasia"]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg/330px-American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barn_owl","wikidata":"Q131528250"},{"title":"Barn","blocks":[{"text":"A barn is an agricultural building usually on farms and used for various purposes. In North America, a barn refers to structures that house livestock, including cattle and horses, as well as equipment and fodder, and often grain. As a result, the term barn is often qualified e.g. tobacco barn, dairy barn, cow house, sheep barn, potato barn. In the British Isles and in Europe, the term barn is restricted mainly to storage structures for unthreshed cereals and fodder, the terms byre, shippon or shippen being applied to cow shelters, whereas horses are kept in buildings known as stables. In mainland Europe, however, barns were often part of integrated structures known as byre-dwellings. In addition, barns may be used for equipment storage, as a covered workplace, and for activities such as threshing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/330px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barn","wikidata":"Q1303167"},{"title":"Owl","blocks":[{"text":"Owls are birds from the order Strigiformes, which includes over 200 species of mostly solitary and nocturnal birds of prey typified by an upright stance, a large, broad head, binocular vision, binaural hearing, sharp talons, and feathers adapted for silent flight. Exceptions include the diurnal northern hawk-owl and the gregarious burrowing owl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG/330px-Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owl","wikidata":"Q25222"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg/500px-American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg/1280px-American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg/500px-American_Barn_Owl_%28Tyto_furcata_guatemalae%29%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American barn owl","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmerican_Barn_Owl_(Tyto_furcata_guatemalae)%2C_Orange_Walk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/500px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/1280px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg/500px-Zalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Timber framed with siding of vertical boards was typical in early New England. The traditional color is the result of iron oxide stain applied to protect the wood from UV damage.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZalmon_Read_Barn_back_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/TTCdairybarn.jpg/330px-TTCdairybarn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/TTCdairybarn.jpg/330px-TTCdairybarn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Texas Technological College Dairy Barn in Lubbock, Texas, U.S., was used as a teaching facility until 1967.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATTCdairybarn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg/500px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg/500px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%93%D1%83%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%BE_1894.jpg?utm_sou